an, documented and preserved Aspasia's medical techniques in his encyclopedic work *Tetrabiblos*."}, {"sourceSlug": "aspasia-the-physician", "sourceName": "Aspasia the Physician", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "women-in-medicine-ancient-greece", "targetName": "Women in Medicine (Ancient Greece)", "context": "Aspasia's documented contributions provided a rare but significant example of female medical expertise, influencing the understanding of women's roles in ancient Greek healthcare."}, {"sourceSlug": "ancient-greek-gynecology", "sourceName": "Ancient Greek Gynecology", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "aspasia-the-physician", "targetName": "Aspasia the Physician", "context": "Aspasia the Physician's specialized knowledge and techniques in women's health were a notable, albeit rare, component of ancient Greek gynecology."}], "places": [{"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Center of Activity"}, {"name": "Byzantium, Eastern Roman Empire", "role": "Source of Documentation"}, {"name": "Ancient Greece", "role": "Cultural Context"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Aspasia the Physician", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "aspasia-the-physician"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q16154352", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aspasia_the_Physician"], "tags": ["Physicians & Medical Pioneers", "innovation-and-technology", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Aspasia the Physician is a crucial figure for understanding the hidden history of women in ancient medicine, as her documented existence challenges prevailing narratives and her techniques were preserved for centuries, influencing later medical encyclopedists.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-16T08:27:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43831115900", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Aspasia the Physician (b. 400 BCE, Athens) was a entertainer or media figure of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Athens. Known in the fields of physicians & medical pioneers.", "newValue": "Aspasia the Physician, active in 4th century BCE Athens, represents a rare documented instance of a female medical practitioner within the intellectual ferment of the Classical Greek world. Her significance lies in her specialized work, primarily in women's health and midwifery, which subtly challenged the prevailing societal norms that largely confined women to domestic roles, offering a distinct, if often overlooked, contribution to the evolving empirical medical science of the Hippocratic tradition.

While direct biographical details are exceptionally scarce, Aspasia's medical expertise is notably referenced by later Byzantine medical writers, most prominently Aëtius of Amida in the 6th century CE. Aëtius, in his monumental medical encyclopedia *Tetrabiblos*, attributes to Aspasia specific gynecological treatments and surgical techniques, including methods for rectifying uterine prolapse, managing uterine inflammation, and performing caesarean sections. These attributions, transmitted through nearly a millennium, suggest a practitioner with practical knowledge and clinical observation skills, likely rooted in the empirical traditions of Athenian medicine, even if the precise details of her original practice remain elusive.

